  "live_records": {
    "q-186809": {
      "dom_id": "step-q186809",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Which of the following number lines represents the inequality $x<-5$ or $x>-3?$",
      "worked_solution": "The inequality $x<-5$ or $x>-3$ means that $x$ can take any value less than $-5,$ or greater than $-3.$\n\nTo represent this on a number line, we graph the two separate inequalities:\n\n- the inequality $x<-5$ consists of an open circle at $-5$ and an arrow to the left.\n\n- the inequality $x>-3$ consists of a open circle at $-3$ and an arrow to the right.\n\nThe resulting graph is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14151668/assets/21bf7706ef220b10f065a8f21c73ea8802740e89f2a5fa5742555f53420899d3.png)",
