    "q-28806": {
      "dom_id": "step-q28806",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Which of the following number lines represents the inequality $x\\le 0$ or $x>\\frac{11}{5}?$",
      "worked_solution": "The inequality $x\\le 0$ or $x>\\frac{11}{5}$ means that $x$ can take any value less than or equal to $0,$ or greater than  $\\frac{11}{5}.$\n\nTo represent this on a number line, we graph the two separate inequalities:\n\n- the inequality $x\\le 0$ consists of a closed circle at $0$ and an arrow to the left.\n\n- the inequality $x>\\frac{11}{5}$ consists of an open circle at $\\frac{11}{5}$ and an arrow to the right.\n\nThe resulting graph is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14151668/assets/32b854cb4bfa0e9d92e044c22275f9ad7c830f621507ee17838d00f650cfe713.png)",
